! The stress of a hydrogen atom in a box at nspin = 2, which is the cell that
! reaches the exchange-correlation energy's saturated branch with real charge on
! it rather than with a fringe.
!
! One electron and one channel: the minority density is 1e-18 to 1e-21 over the
! whole grid, so wherever the majority density is large enough to swamp it in
! float64 the test |rho_up - rho_dw| >= |rho_up + rho_dw| holds exactly. That is
! 863 of 64,000 points here and they carry 0.597 of the one electron, meaning the
! saturated branch is the core of the atom rather than the vacuum around it.
!
! Why the stress and not the force. At frozen wavefunctions the density follows
! the geometry only through a core or an augmentation charge, and this dataset
! has neither, so the force cannot see the branch at all; under a strain the
! whole density moves, since rho carries 1/Omega, so the strain derivative reads
! d(rho e_xc)/drho at every point. With that derivative masked the diagonal came
! out at +6.65e-05 Ry/bohr^3 against pw.x's -1.049e-05, which is 11.3 kbar and
! the opposite sign, and the total energy was right to every digit throughout.
!
! The other spin-polarized stress in the suite is pw_lsda/lsda.in, nickel, whose
! two channels are both populated everywhere, so not one of its points is
! saturated and it could not have shown this.
 &control
    calculation = 'scf'
    tstress = .true.
    tprnfor = .true.
 /
 &system
    ibrav = 1, celldm(1) = 12.0,
    nat = 1, ntyp = 1,
    ecutwfc = 25.0,
    occupations = 'smearing', smearing = 'gaussian', degauss = 0.02
    nspin = 2
    starting_magnetization(1) = 0.6
    nbnd = 4
 /
 &electrons
    mixing_beta = 0.3
    conv_thr = 1.0d-10
 /
ATOMIC_SPECIES
 H  1.008  H.pz-vbc.UPF
ATOMIC_POSITIONS (alat)
 H 0.0 0.0 0.0
K_POINTS {automatic}
 1 1 1 0 0 0
